// openjoan.exe - standalone launcher (docs/RUNTIME.md, docs/RUNTIME.md).
//
// The game runs as this executable. joan.exe is not opened at all: everything it contained that the game still
// needs is part of openjoan.exe -
//   - its data (.rdata / .data / .bss / .idata) is ONE object of ours, joan_image (src/data/image.cpp, typed and
//     named C++ generated by re/gen_data_curated.py), at its original relative offsets. Before any other static
//     constructor runs, its stored data pointers are relocated to where it really is and its contents are checked
//     against joan.exe's bytes (CRC-32 per section; src/image_runtime.cpp, loader/data_image.cpp). Stage S7b.
//   - its code is our C++ (linked in). Every original code address our C++ or the data image still uses is a slot of
//     the code table joan_code[] (src/code_table.cpp), filled while the replacements are installed; the data image's
//     code pointers (vtables, CRT initialisers, callbacks) are pointed at their slots' targets before the game runs.
//     Nothing exists at joan.exe's old code addresses any more. Stage S7c.
//   - its imports (src/import_table.inc) are resolved here into the IAT slots inside joan_image. Imports with an entry
//     in the override table (loader/import_overrides.hpp) get our function instead of the DLL's export, and a DLL
//     whose imports are all overridden is never loaded: v3d_87.dll (all 23, render/v3d) and DINPUT8.dll (our
//     DirectInput8Create, which fails: DirectInput is not used); KERNEL32's ExitProcess is ours too. An import that
//     nothing in src/ can reach any more (loader/dead_imports.inc, generated by re/gen_dead_imports.py) and that has
//     no active override gets a trap (reports "dead import <dll>!<name> called" and ends the game) instead of the
//     DLL's export, so a DLL whose imports are all overridden or dead is not loaded either.
//     Its resources (OpenJoan's own icon 102 and version information, ADR-0002) are in joan_re.rc.
// Steps:
//   1. reports the data image's startup check / relocation (done before any other static constructor),
//   2. installs the stray guard (src/image_runtime.cpp: a fault at one of joan.exe's old addresses 0x00400000..
//      0x00a96000 where nothing is mapped is logged with its data symbol / code slot and caller, and ends the game),
//   3. resolves the imports into the IAT inside joan_image (override table first, then LoadLibrary / GetProcAddress),
//   4. installs every C++ replacement and seals the code table (host.cpp), points the data image's code pointers at
//      their targets and makes .rdata read-only,
//   5. calls joan.exe's CRT entry point (WinMainCRTStartup, our C++ port) through its code slot.
//
// Stage S7d: nothing of joan.exe is at a fixed address any more. openjoan.exe is linked /DYNAMICBASE /NXCOMPAT (ASLR and
// DEP): its base differs from run to run, and joan_image / joan_code move with it. Nothing is reserved at joan.exe's
// old range (no relaunch, no synthetic PE header at 0x00400000): GetModuleHandle(NULL) - the game's instance handle -
// is openjoan.exe, whose resources are our own (joan_re.rc: icon 102, version information; ADR-0002).
//
// openjoan.ini [Loader]:
//   PoisonDead=1      fill the left-out dead ranges of the data image (src/data/dead.cpp) with 0xCD instead of zero -
//                     a game run then proves that nothing reads them.
//   (Code=, Data=, StrayDataGuard=: retired keys, named in openjoan.log by registry.cpp load_settings.)
